[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No current fault on router16. OSPF on router16 is running normally and forwarding traffic between Campus 1 and Campus 2/backbone.

Mechanism check: If router16’s OSPF had died, Campus 1 VLANs (172.16.10/20/30) would lose routes to 10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24, and backbone networks, causing ping failures. Instead, multiple cross-campus probes succeed: pc5 (172.16.10.5) to pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc3 (10.6.0.3) both 0% loss; pc10 (172.16.20.10) to pc3 also 0% loss; pc12 to pc1 0% loss. Campus 1 also reaches the backbone: pc5 to router10 (192.168.144.1) 0% loss.

Localization and control-plane evidence on router16: OSPF neighbors are Full on both uplinks (bond0 to 10.2.2.2 and eth2 to 172.30.1.2). “show ip ospf neighbor” shows two fully adjacent neighbors; “show ip route” contains OSPF-learned routes for 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 via 172.30.1.2 and for Campus 1 VLANs via 10.2.2.2. OSPF SPF ran recently and is inactive now—typical for a healthy process.

Root cause: No active edge fault found on router16; at most a brief adjacency reset occurred a couple of minutes before testing (neighbor uptime ~2 minutes), but service is restored.

Corrective fix: None required at present. Recommend monitoring FRR logs on router16 for recent restarts or link flaps and enabling telemetry/alerts on OSPF neighbor state to catch transient issues if they recur. === END ===